---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About | Prashant Shah">
  <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.8rem,4vw,2.4rem)] mb-10" transition:name="page-title">About</h1>

  <div class="space-y-6 text-[15px] text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-[600px]">
    <p>
      I grew up speaking Bengali at home, English at school, and enough Hindi to follow along when my parents switched mid-sentence. Three languages before I was ten. That does something to how you parse ambiguity. You learn early that the same idea wears different clothes depending on who's hearing it. I think about that a lot when I'm building software for people who aren't engineers.
    </p>
    <p>
      I came to Ramapo College for computer science and stayed because the scale let me do things you can't do at a bigger school. I co-founded the CS club and convinced Bjarne Stroustrup to come speak. I managed 17 resident assistants and a community of 200+ students. I led the math club, competed in ICPC at the New York regional, and still had time to actually build things. The range isn't a resume filler. It's the reason I know how to talk to a stakeholder who doesn't speak code, scope a project for a team that's never worked together, and debug a system while someone's waiting on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm finishing a double major in Computer Science and Data Science with a minor in Mathematics. My GPA is a 3.84. I've interned at Trimble, Novartis, and the Center for Food Action. My research under Dr. Al-Juboori is about what happens when user intent contradicts content semantics in retrieval systems. The work I care about most is the work that helps people who weren't going to be helped otherwise: pantry volunteers logging donations one-handed, students who can't parse their own LMS data, users whose emotional needs don't match what an algorithm thinks they want.
    </p>
    <p>
      I want to be the kind of engineer who understands the system end to end: the data, the infrastructure, the interface, and the person on the other side of it. Not a specialist in one layer. Someone who can look at a problem from the user's chair and trace it all the way back to the query plan.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-16 pt-10 border-t border-[var(--color-border)]">
    <h2 class="text-[14px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--color-faint)] mb-6">Things That Don't Fit on a Resume</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-4 text-[15px] text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <li>
        <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Canstruction (1st place)</span> &mdash; Built a structure entirely from canned food as part of a design competition. It's one of the stranger things I've done and one of the most fun.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Student Employee of the Year</span> &mdash; For the RA work. Managing a residential community while carrying a double major taught me more about time management and people than any course.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Presidential Scholarship recipient</span> &mdash; Full four years at Ramapo.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-[600px]">
      Every system I've built started with watching someone struggle with the one it replaced. That's not a philosophy statement. It's just how the work happens.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
